{"id":"rotting-oranges","title":"Rotting Oranges","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"Graphs","tags":["Array","BFS","Matrix"],"companies":{"google":3,"apple":3,"meta":3,"amazon":5,"microsoft":3},"description":"An `m x n` grid holds `0` (empty), `1` (fresh orange) or `2` (rotten orange). Every minute, each fresh orange that is 4-adjacent to a rotten one becomes rotten.\n\nReturn the number of minutes until no fresh orange remains. If some fresh orange can never rot, return `-1`. A grid with no fresh oranges needs `0` minutes.","examples":[{"input":"grid = [[2,1,1],[1,1,0],[0,1,1]]","output":"4","explanation":"The rot spreads one ring per minute; the orange at (2,2) is the last to turn, after 4 minutes."},{"input":"grid = [[2,1,1],[0,1,1],[1,0,1]]","output":"-1","explanation":"The fresh orange at (2,0) is walled off by empty cells and never rots."},{"input":"grid = [[0,2]]","output":"0","explanation":"Nothing fresh to begin with."}],"constraints":["0 <= m, n <= 50","grid[i][j] is 0, 1 or 2"],"hints":["All rotten oranges spread simultaneously. Which traversal processes cells in order of distance from a set of sources?","Multi-source BFS: enqueue every rotten orange at time 0, then expand level by level, each level being one minute.","Count the fresh oranges up front; decrement as they rot. If the count is not zero at the end, return -1. The answer is the number of levels that actually rotted something."],"function":"orangesRotting","signature":"def orangesRotting(self, grid: List[List[int]]) -> int:","arg_types":{"grid":"List[List[int]]"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"grid":[[2,1,1],[1,1,0],[0,1,1]]},"output":4},{"input":{"grid":[[2,1,1],[0,1,1],[1,0,1]]},"output":-1},{"input":{"grid":[[0,2]]},"output":0},{"input":{"grid":[]},"output":0},{"input":{"grid":[[1]]},"output":-1},{"input":{"grid":[[2]]},"output":0},{"input":{"grid":[[0]]},"output":0},{"input":{"grid":[[1,2,1]]},"output":1},{"input":{"grid":[[2,0,1]]},"output":-1},{"input":{"grid":[[2